> `lifecycle_msgs/*` is auto-injected by Rclex's generator, so it does not
> need to appear in `:ros2_message_types` / `:ros2_service_types`.

## About

A managed node implements the [ROS 2 lifecycle design](https://design.ros.org/articles/node_lifecycle.html):
the four primary states `unconfigured → inactive → active → finalized` plus
the standard `change_state` / `get_state` services.

Reference implementations:

* C++ — [ros2/demos · `lifecycle/src/lifecycle_talker.cpp`](https://github.com/ros2/demos/blob/rolling/lifecycle/src/lifecycle_talker.cpp)
  and [`lifecycle_listener.cpp`](https://github.com/ros2/demos/blob/rolling/lifecycle/src/lifecycle_listener.cpp)
* Python — [ros2/demos · `lifecycle_py/lifecycle_py/talker.py`](https://github.com/ros2/demos/blob/rolling/lifecycle_py/lifecycle_py/talker.py)
* Design doc — [Managed Nodes](https://design.ros.org/articles/node_lifecycle.html)
* See also `Rclex.LifecycleNode` in this repo.

Verify from another terminal:

```sh
ros2 lifecycle nodes
ros2 lifecycle get /managed_talker
ros2 lifecycle set /managed_talker configure
ros2 lifecycle set /managed_talker activate
```

